    "q-71043": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71043",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-4)}{0}=\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+4}{0}=\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+4}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-4:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5+3t \\\\ 1+2t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+4}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-4$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{2},\\,x=-4\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-71043-a",
