    "q-127408": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127408",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line.",
      "worked_solution": "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,-3,7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-5,2,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-5}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{2}=\\frac{z-7}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-5}=\\frac{y+3}{2}=\\frac{z-7}{-1}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-127408-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{x-2}{-1}=\\frac{y+3}{2}=\\frac{z-7}{-5}",
