    "q-127468": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127468",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14151155/assets/01943e80947c833ef37241fb836db96afd61731b9873cd061277a7453b34f3d8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above.",
      "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,3,7)$ and $Q(3,-1,1).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩-⟨-1,3,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-127468-a",
