    "q-127527": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127527",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14151155/assets/8722b93655e069e2f35147b572c16ed7af408a86f8a88597c79aea8fe59e9c4d.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above.",
      "worked_solution": "We know that the line passes through the points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-3,3⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3.$ For example, our two points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=3.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ part and instead write $z=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
